WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.780
NEAL MUELLER: Hello, everyone,
and welcome to the third

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.290
in a series of three
on-air programs.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.299
The first was about
getting started with GCP

00:00:17.299 --> 00:00:18.590
and what you can build quickly.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.730
The second was about
machine learning

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.190
and how you can inspire
love in your customers.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.210
The third is about
bringing GCP close to you.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.300
My name is Neal Mueller.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.634
I work on the Geographic
Expansion team of GCP.

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:30.300
DAVE STIVER: Hi.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.310
I'm Dave Stiver.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.320
I'm a PM for our
geo-expansion efforts.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.330
I've been at Google
for the last 12 years,

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.610
really building out the
compute storage and networking

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:38.270
infrastructure.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.110
NEAL MUELLER: That's
a long time, Dave.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:39.860
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.980
It's part of what we really
use to bring GCP to you.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.280
NEAL MUELLER: Great.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.030
Let's get started.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.490
We've got a bunch of content.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.530
Before I get started,
I want to tell you

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.600
that you can leave
questions for us

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.410
anytime up until the
end of our presentation.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.317
We're going to answer all your
questions at the very end.

00:00:57.317 --> 00:00:58.900
We'll get to them
for sure, try to get

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.470
to every single one of them.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.192
We're going to talk
for about 30 minutes,

00:01:02.192 --> 00:01:04.650
and then we're going to answer
your questions for about 15.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:05.390
OK.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:06.181
DAVE STIVER: Great.

00:01:06.181 --> 00:01:07.640
NEAL MUELLER: Let's get going.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.870
DAVE STIVER: So just a
little bit of context.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:09.786
Why are we doing this?

00:01:09.786 --> 00:01:11.720
Why are we putting regions
all over the world?

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.430
There are really three
main requirements

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:15.060
that we look at for customers.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.730
The number one reason
is performance.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:18.690
They're looking for low
latency applications.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.930
So we have to put our
infrastructure close to you

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.530
and close your customers.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.190
The second reason is
high availability.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:28.070
They want multiple locations to
select from in order for them

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.890
to meet their business
continuity needs

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.360
and build highly
available applications.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.090
And then the last one
is really control.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.340
We put these locations in
different parts of the world

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.140
because customers
really want control

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.820
over where they build
their applications

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.420
and where they store their data.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.750
It's really important that
we bring the product to you.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.880
And you, as customers,
get to pick where

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.192
you want your data to live.

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:51.107
NEAL MUELLER: OK.

00:01:51.107 --> 00:01:53.690
DAVE STIVER: So if you look at
this infrastructure investment,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.892
Google has really been a cloud
company for the last decade.

00:01:56.892 --> 00:01:58.850
But if you think about
what cloud is doing even

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.670
at Google's scale, we
made a huge investment

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.320
in our infrastructure just
in the last three years.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.380
If you look at just the
last three-year spend,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.710
it's been over $30 billion,
which even at Google's scale

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:10.919
is a pretty large investment.

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:12.876
So this isn't something
small that we're doing.

00:02:12.876 --> 00:02:14.960
It's something that's
fundamental to the company.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.376
NEAL MUELLER: As
Eric Schmidt said

00:02:16.376 --> 00:02:18.350
in our last Next in San
Francisco last summer,

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.925
we built a cloud this big
so that you don't have to.

00:02:20.925 --> 00:02:26.180
To give you an idea about how
big our cloud looks physically,

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.110
let's bring ourselves
to the Netherlands.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.040
So Dave and I just launched our
new region in the Netherlands

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:31.541
last month.

00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:33.206
DAVE STIVER: Just a
couple of weeks ago.

00:02:33.206 --> 00:02:34.370
NEAL MUELLER: Not long ago.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.710
Although I didn't get a chance
to see this data center,

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.440
I had to share a couple
of the pictures with you.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.900
To give you an idea, this is
our Netherlands data center,

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.566
which has a variety
of Google services--

00:02:43.566 --> 00:02:46.940
Google search, YouTube, a
number of other services,

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.280
and then a GCP region
inside it as well.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.170
You can see in the
distance some windmills

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.820
that are used to power this
region with renewable energy.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.630
Security is something
we take very seriously.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.974
This is what the exterior
security looks like.

00:03:01.974 --> 00:03:03.640
As you can see, the
security guard shack

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.223
is covered with
grass, which I thought

00:03:05.223 --> 00:03:07.600
was particularly beautiful.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.339
On the inside, there
are different cells

00:03:09.339 --> 00:03:11.380
of the data center which
separate different cells

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.250
from each other.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.070
We operate the products
within Google very separately.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.680
You can see we've got a mantrap
here with an IRA scanner

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:18.997
and a physical guard shack.

00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:20.830
DAVE STIVER: Just one
note on that, I think,

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.780
to build on what
Neal said is it's

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.850
important to understand
we have data centers all

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.460
over the world, but we use
the same system of physical

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.440
and logical security
in order to make sure

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:30.557
that your data stays safe.

00:03:30.557 --> 00:03:32.890
It looks a little bit different
at the data center level

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:35.170
wherever you go in the world,
but it's the same model

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.376
that we use everywhere.

00:03:37.376 --> 00:03:40.310
NEAL MUELLER: Nice.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:40.849
OK.

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:42.640
I mentioned something
about the environment

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.200
and how we use
renewable energy and how

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.680
we've reached 100%
renewable energy to power

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.231
our data centers globally.

00:03:49.231 --> 00:03:50.980
Another thing that's
kind of interesting--

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.854
and I'll let you guys
in on a little secret--

00:03:52.854 --> 00:03:56.290
is that when we first built this
data center a couple of years

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.960
ago, we discovered from
ecologists that we were

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.430
going to disrupt a bat habitat.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:04.840
So our local cyber reliability
engineers and local officials

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.460
teamed up together to build this
thing that we call a bat cave.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.024
This bat cave houses
a habitat of bats

00:04:11.024 --> 00:04:12.940
that you can see in the
upper left-hand corner

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:13.750
of this picture.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.899
They're about the
size of your finger.

00:04:15.899 --> 00:04:17.440
And with the result
of this bat cave,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.231
their habitat wasn't
disrupted, and we were

00:04:19.231 --> 00:04:22.390
able to build this data center.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:24.435
If the data center filling
up is any indication

00:04:24.435 --> 00:04:26.560
of how much the bats are
going to start filling up,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.360
we'll soon have to
build another bat cave.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:29.110
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:04:31.394 --> 00:04:33.560
NEAL MUELLER: This is a
picture of a solar farm very

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.930
close to the Netherlands
data center that

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:39.430
produces enough solar energy
to power the Netherlands data

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.854
center.

00:04:40.854 --> 00:04:42.770
You can see the windmills
in the distance too.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.490
A note on energy.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.097
Not only have we reached
100% renewable energy

00:04:51.097 --> 00:04:53.430
by either consuming renewable
energy itself or by buying

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.360
enough offsets, but we also use
less energy in our data centers

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.950
than the industry average.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.551
The industry average PUE, which
is a measure of effectiveness,

00:05:02.551 --> 00:05:05.050
how much energy we're consuming
versus how much energy we're

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:05.790
wasting--

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:08.220
the industry average is 1.7.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.990
Google's global average
across all of our data centers

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.340
is 1.12.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.690
That's much, much less.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.180
2 is bad, and 1 is really great.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:20.827
1 means that every energy
that you're taking in,

00:05:20.827 --> 00:05:22.410
you're using to power
the data center.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.840
2 means that for every piece
of energy you're taking in,

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.670
you're wasting an
equivalent atom of energy

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:27.670
at the same time.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.245
So we're 1.12, and the
industry average is 1.7.

00:05:30.245 --> 00:05:32.370
This means that we can
build data centers like this

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.320
that operate at a
very high performance

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.870
but consume
appreciably less energy

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:38.490
than any other data
centers that we've

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.820
been able to find in the world.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.903
DAVE STIVER: There's a
really interesting parallel

00:05:41.903 --> 00:05:44.340
there, too, to draw between
the data center infrastructure

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.092
and the cloud world.

00:05:46.092 --> 00:05:47.550
Things that are
important to Google

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.290
are being opened
with our technology.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.450
And so when we were
doing all that work back

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:55.650
in 2008, 2009 in order to
improve our data center

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.870
efficiency, we got done
with all that work,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:00.180
and then we published
all of that externally.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:01.560
We shared it with the world.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.840
We essentially wrote the
script or the playbook

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.600
for how other people could get
that kind of center efficiency.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:07.410
So it's one of the proudest--

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.430
I was building data center
infrastructure at that time,

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.179
so it's really one of
the proudest moments

00:06:11.179 --> 00:06:13.380
I had that we took
all of that IP

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.870
and made it publicly
available to everybody.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.970
The corollary, really,
in the cloud world

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.470
is something like
Kubernetes, where

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.320
we take a really fundamental
core technology and then

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.880
open source it.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.179
So open source is
super critical to us.

00:06:26.179 --> 00:06:27.720
We're committed to
that as a company,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.890
both at the data center
infrastructure level

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:30.936
and at the cloud level.

00:06:30.936 --> 00:06:31.686
NEAL MUELLER: Wow.

00:06:31.686 --> 00:06:33.887
Great.

00:06:33.887 --> 00:06:35.970
DAVE STIVER: So if we think
a little bit about how

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.340
we layer cloud over the
top of all that data

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.980
center infrastructure, we can
talk a couple of dimensions.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:44.040
And the first dimension is
probably cloud locations.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.540
So we have multiple
versions of this

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.730
that you can think of to
choose from as a customer,

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.780
and we'll build
from the inside out.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:51.150
So we'll start with zones.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.550
You can think of zones as
independent failure domains

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.029
within a region.

00:06:55.029 --> 00:06:57.070
So they're independent
across multiple dimensions

00:06:57.070 --> 00:06:58.695
of the physical
infrastructure, so they

00:06:58.695 --> 00:07:01.350
have isolation across
power, cooling, networking.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.470
And probably most
importantly, the control plane

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:05.940
is isolated as well
in these zones.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.340
So you can choose
these individually.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:10.610
And then if you put a
couple of zones together--

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:12.950
usually we have three
zones per region.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:16.710
You combine a couple of them
together, and you get a region.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.690
So regions are a geographically
independent location.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:22.772
So an example of one of our
oldest regions is in Iowa,

00:07:22.772 --> 00:07:24.480
or we have one in
Belgium, or one that we

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.730
launched last year in Tokyo.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.330
Those are all cloud regions
that customers can choose.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.600
Then beyond that, if you combine
multiple regions together,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.460
you get what's called
a multi-region.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.280
And that's a product offering we
have where you can choose that.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.680
And essentially, we
combine multiple regions

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.950
that are geographically
separate from one another

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:46.170
together into a
product offering that

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.540
gives you that level
of isolation and higher

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:50.010
availability.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.720
And then the last one is global.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.480
Global is a service that's
essentially spread everywhere

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.939
in the world where Google data
center infrastructure lives.

00:07:56.939 --> 00:07:58.813
So that's something
where you don't even have

00:07:58.813 --> 00:08:00.210
to think about the location.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.190
It's just automatically spread
everywhere in the world.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:04.830
And I think it's
worth maybe walking

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.500
through a couple of examples
of not just the locations,

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.620
but now how do the services
deploy on top of those.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:11.610
Does that make sense?

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:11.960
NEAL MUELLER: Yeah.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:12.576
Let's do it.

00:08:12.576 --> 00:08:13.430
DAVE STIVER: Great.

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:16.290
So a couple of the product or
service deployment models--

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:18.180
I mentioned we have
regional offerings.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.870
So something like Compute Engine
is a regional or zonal product.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.770
You can choose a zone in
any region around the world.

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:28.480
Compute Engine is fundamental
to infrastructure GCP.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.180
So you can pick in
Oregon or Northern

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.350
Virginia, all of our locations.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:37.240
Another example is our Object
Storage product cloud storage.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.820
It has both a regional and
a multi-regional offering.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:43.260
So you can choose today
a bucket in the Americas

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.300
or in Europe or in Asia.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:46.860
And when you upload
your data, we

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.960
will automatically
replicate that data

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.810
to those different regions for
that multi-regional offering

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.330
to give you the
higher availability.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.820
And then the last
offering is global.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.080
We have a product like
Pub/Sub, our messaging service,

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.140
which is inherently
everywhere in the world.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:05.400
So that data and those
messages get spread everywhere

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.280
where we have infrastructure and
have Pub/Sub running depending

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.239
on where your customers are.

00:09:11.239 --> 00:09:12.030
NEAL MUELLER: Good.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:13.656
DAVE STIVER: Three
different offerings.

00:09:13.656 --> 00:09:15.321
NEAL MUELLER: So let's
talk about a tool

00:09:15.321 --> 00:09:17.350
that customers can use
if they've got questions.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.360
DAVE STIVER: Yeah,
don't take it for me.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.190
NEAL MUELLER: So we'll
leave the slides,

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.179
and we'll go to the
actual internet.

00:09:22.179 --> 00:09:23.970
We're going to navigate
to a website that's

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:24.970
visible to all you guys.

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.620
It's cloud.google.com
/about/locations.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.750
The top of the website
looks like this.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:32.970
But if you scroll
down past the map,

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:36.160
you're going to see what
Dave and I call the matrix.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:37.814
So let's walk them
through the matrix.

00:09:37.814 --> 00:09:39.980
DAVE STIVER: So we have it
broken up by each market.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.510
Today, it's the Americas,
it's Europe, and Asia-Pacific.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:44.010
As we continue to
add regions, we'll

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.130
add more continents, probably.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:48.620
But let's look at two services,
just so you can quickly

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:49.250
get an example.

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:52.500
Each column is a region, and
then each row is a service.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.100
So Compute Engine,
as I mentioned,

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.930
is kind of a fundamental
core product in GCP.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:00.230
Today, it's already available
in every single region in GCP.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:01.160
And that's the goal.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.530
That's where we want to
get, where as much of GCP

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:06.070
is available in
every single region

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:08.390
that we launched that
we have available today.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:11.120
If you drop down
and look in Storage,

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:14.300
see something like
Cloud Bigtable.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.780
Super popular product,
newer than Compute Engine.

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.730
It's actually trailing
a little bit behind.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:23.630
We launch regions faster than
