00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:18.350
NEAL MUELLER: Nice.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:18.850
OK.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.680
So we've talked
about the 15 regions

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:25.240
where we serve GCP customers.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.610
You can think of these
into three big buckets--

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.850
Americas, Europe,
and Asia-Pacific.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.405
On the x-axis here, we have
all of these 15 regions,

00:11:33.405 --> 00:11:35.530
starting with Iowa and
ending all the way over here

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:36.520
with Sydney.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:40.900
On the y-axis, we have how
much this costs for cores.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:42.940
You could do this for
storage, or for big data,

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:44.980
or for machine
learning, or networking.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.860
It would follow a very
similar trend line.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:49.990
What you're seeing
here is that the cost

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:53.406
of providing GCP services
to you in the Americas

00:11:53.406 --> 00:11:55.780
is a little bit less than in
Europe and a little bit less

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.160
in Asia-Pacific.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:59.170
This is true across all
of the cloud providers

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:00.910
and also within Google.

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.370
Why is that, Dave?

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:04.300
DAVE STIVER: So part
of it is just the cost

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:05.560
of doing business varies.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:08.185
The cost of energy is different
in each one of these locations,

00:12:08.185 --> 00:12:09.400
and the cost of the land.

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.470
Part of it is the
age of the facility.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:12.970
So there are
efficiencies that you

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.340
get the longer that
facility is up and running.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.840
And the last one is
just economies of scale.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:20.280
So you can see here if you
look, the oldest regions

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:21.280
are the largest regions.

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:23.750
And as we grow and get
more and more customers,

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:25.240
we get economies of scale.

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.020
And that allows
us to drop prices.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:30.520
NEAL MUELLER: So what you can
see here is as a GCP customer,

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.690
you can pick wherever you
want to run workloads.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.897
If you want the workload
to be very close to you,

00:12:34.897 --> 00:12:37.480
you're going to benefit from the
speed of light benefits of it

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:38.975
being very nearby you.

00:12:38.975 --> 00:12:41.350
If you're willing to let it
be a little bit further away,

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:42.290
you can go for--

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:43.998
you're now empowered
with the information

00:12:43.998 --> 00:12:47.230
then to know where to buy that
for the least amount of money.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:50.501
But I'd be remiss if I didn't
talk a bit about pricing

00:12:50.501 --> 00:12:52.000
as compared to other
cloud providers

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.500
as long as we're
talking about us.

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:58.374
So we have a number of pricing
philosophies within GCP

00:12:58.374 --> 00:13:00.040
that are very
advantageous to customers.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:02.320
We call it
customer-friendly pricing.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.880
One of them is called Sustained
Use Discounts, which I

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:06.910
abbreviated in this slide SUD.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.650
Now, what sustained
use discount says

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:11.380
is if you're going to
use a piece of Compute

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:16.692
for 25% or more of the month,
you are granted a discount.

00:13:16.692 --> 00:13:17.650
This is just automatic.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:19.840
You don't have to apply
for it or send any emails,

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:20.890
make any phone calls.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:22.810
You just get it on your bill.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.430
If you use it for the
entirety of the month,

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:27.610
you can get up to
a 30% discount.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:30.310
So think if you use it for 30
days, you get a 30% discount.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:32.380
It's a nice, easy mnemonic.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.360
So you can see our list
prices are listed here

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.250
in the height of the blue bar.

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:38.410
If you subtract
from that 30%, you

00:13:38.410 --> 00:13:41.110
will see the SUD price,
the Sustained Use Discount.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.390
Now, just as a
comparison, I've posted

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:46.060
what Amazon's price was
as of a couple of days

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:47.480
ago when I made this slide.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.190
And you can see what
it is right here.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:53.740
So you can see that
the general price

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:58.360
increases from America to Europe
and Asia is also with Amazon.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.150
And in general, we
are much, much less.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:04.360
When you account for
Sustained Use Discount,

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.792
we are appreciably less than
other public cloud providers,

00:14:07.792 --> 00:14:08.500
including Amazon.

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:15.370
DAVE STIVER: So just to
give a little bit of context

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:19.060
to how we've grown, if you
look back, I think, 18 months,

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:22.610
we had four regions
around the world.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.910
And today we have 15.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:28.870
So that's, like, 300% growth
in the last 18 months,

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:31.640
which even at Google
scale is significant.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:33.610
You can see all of
our launch regions

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:34.954
