00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:09.940
so 150 milliseconds to Mumbai.

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:10.440
OK.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:11.260
Back to the slides.

00:16:17.541 --> 00:16:18.040
There, GCP.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:20.000
DAVE STIVER: Yep.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:23.790
Just to give you a little bit
of context visually for how we--

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:26.030
what this launch
velocity looks like,

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:28.580
one of the things that we
decided maybe two years ago

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:33.530
was we just weren't keeping pace
with the speed of our customers

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:34.790
and what they needed for us.

00:16:34.790 --> 00:16:36.440
So we spent almost
a year, really,

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.870
building out a set
of automation tools

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:42.240
to allow us to quickly
turn up a new region.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.870
So everything in our
stack, from the lowest

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.420
level of infrastructure--
every DIM,

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:49.640
every server, every
rack all the way up

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.620
to the top of the stack, our
managed services offering,

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:56.030
have all been automated
so that we can stand up

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.935
a set of new zones and
a region in a relatively

00:16:59.935 --> 00:17:00.810
short amount of time.

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.200
So you can see what our
launch velocity look

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:08.599
like there in 2015 and 2016,
what we've been up to in 2017,

00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:11.192
and then what we have
in store in 2018.

00:17:11.192 --> 00:17:12.650
NEAL MUELLER: So
your automation is

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:15.200
why you were able to tumble
them out so quick in 2017?

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:15.380
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.671
Without that, we never would
have been able to do this.

00:17:17.671 --> 00:17:20.929
We'd be talking about 7 or
8 or 10 regions, not 15.

00:17:20.929 --> 00:17:22.970
NEAL MUELLER: And from
what I'm hearing from you,

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:24.916
it's probably going
to just continue.

00:17:24.916 --> 00:17:25.790
DAVE STIVER: Correct.

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:26.748
NEAL MUELLER: OK, good.

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:30.300
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:33.210
For each one of those new
region launches, as I mentioned,

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.520
we're trying to put every
service in all locations.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.370
But right now, this is the
standard offering that we have.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:41.130
And it cuts across all
of the dimensions of GCP,

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:44.150
whether it's compute,
storage, big data, networking,

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:45.430
or IAAM services.

00:17:45.430 --> 00:17:47.820
It's something that we
put into every location.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:50.070
I did highlight a few
services because we just

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:51.690
finished the work on those.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.120
Cloud Spanner and
Cloud Bigtable will now

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.280
be a part of every new
region launch going forward,

00:17:57.280 --> 00:17:58.770
so you won't have
to wait for that.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:01.320
Cloud BigQuery has
extensive plans

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.719
to roll out to more
locations in 2018,

00:18:03.719 --> 00:18:05.260
and we'll get them
caught up shortly.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:10.410
So that critical key feature
of GCP Cloud BigQuery

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:12.390
will be available
everywhere for you.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:13.710
NEAL MUELLER: You're going
to put Spanner, Bigtable,

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:15.660
and BigQuery in every
single new region?

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:17.940
DAVE STIVER: That's what
we said 18 months ago.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:19.680
How would we possibly do that?

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.780
Automation is the
way that we get that.

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:25.117
NEAL MUELLER: So we can expect
Hong Kong, Osaka, Finland, LA.

00:18:25.117 --> 00:18:27.450
All of them will have those
three that you bolded there?

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:28.450
DAVE STIVER: Absolutely.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.370
NEAL MUELLER: Fantastic.

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:33.000
DAVE STIVER: You
want me to take this?

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:33.530
NEAL MUELLER: You can start.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:34.030
I'll finish.

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:35.030
DAVE STIVER: OK.

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:36.762
So we talk a lot about regions.

00:18:36.762 --> 00:18:37.970
We talk a lot about services.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:40.303
But one of the things that
we don't, sometimes, I think,

00:18:40.303 --> 00:18:42.460
talk about enough
is the investment

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:46.050
that we've made in our
network infrastructure.

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:48.770
We've been building
out this global network

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:51.200
for over the last
15 years, really.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.990
And it's kind of the
secret sauce of Google.

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:57.180
We have an expression a decade
ago almost now that said,

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.640
it's all about the network.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:18:59.510
And it really is.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:01.250
It's the tie that binds.

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:04.550
So you take this network
infrastructure investment,

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.470
combine it with all
the regions, combine it

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:09.440
with the edge caches and the
points of presence as we have,

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:12.830
and it really becomes
this global scale computer

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:14.670
that you can program on top of.

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:16.760
It's what all of our
engineers here at Google

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:17.760
program on top of it.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:19.651
It's what they've
become accustomed to.

00:19:19.651 --> 00:19:20.150
Be careful.

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:21.680
Once you get used to
how fast it is-- you

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:23.480
talked about how much
better the latency is.

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:23.980
Be careful.

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:25.880
Once you get used
to how fast it is,

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.470
it's hard to go back
to the public internet.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:32.210
But this is really a significant
part of that infrastructure

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:32.711
investment.

00:19:32.711 --> 00:19:34.376
NEAL MUELLER: The
only thing I would add

00:19:34.376 --> 00:19:36.170
is if you've ever
used Google.com

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:39.260
and the brilliance
of search, you

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:40.860
have experienced this network.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:42.920
It's the distributed
computing problem

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:45.740
that Google solved long
before I joined Google.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:48.350
It was solved with
a network like this.

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:50.390
And it'll empower
your business to solve

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:54.770
incredibly delightful and joyous
user experiences like we've

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:56.236
done with google.com.

00:20:00.520 --> 00:20:01.604
DAVE STIVER: OK.

00:20:01.604 --> 00:20:02.520
What's the expression?

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:05.830
The expression is
politics is local.

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:07.140
So all politics is local.

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:08.310
All energy is local.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:09.270
I like to say that.

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.040
All cloud is really local too.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:12.900
So these regions
launches are not

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.730
just about putting
data centers in places.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.610
They're really about us bringing
GCP and meeting customers

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:18.810
where they're at.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:21.780
So we've been out launching
these regions for the last 18

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:23.640
months, going to
different locations.

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:26.680
And it's more than
just a data center.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:29.070
It's a lot more than
that in terms of opening

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:30.010
p a new market.

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:30.801
NEAL MUELLER: Yeah.

00:20:30.801 --> 00:20:33.720
It's about helping
local customers

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:37.170
with local salespeople and
local tech support people

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:38.790
and engaging local engineers.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:41.491
Because Google has engineers
all over the world.

00:20:41.491 --> 00:20:42.990
To give you a flavor
for this, we've

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.320
got two animated
GIFs and one photo.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.196
So let's just start with
the one on the left.

00:20:47.196 --> 00:20:47.695
OK.

00:20:47.695 --> 00:20:49.740
So the one on the left is
about our German region

00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:52.590
launch, which happened
right around Oktoberfest

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:53.520
of last year.

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:54.270
It was incredible.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:55.870
On the left in that
picture, you see

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.590
Urs Holzle, who is Google's
first vice president.

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:01.440
He's been at Google
a long time--

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:03.270
16, 18, years, long time.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.630
He's heavily engaged with the
geographic expansion of GCP.

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:08.550
On his left is
Michael Korbacher,

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:12.540
who's in charge of the customer
team in Germany and Austria

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:13.440
and Switzerland.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:15.240
And they're pressing
a buzzer in front

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:19.260
of an audience of 300 customers
as they opened the first region

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:20.730
in Germany.

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:22.170
It was really exciting.

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:24.900
In the middle, we've got
someone that I recognize.

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:27.330
Dave Stiver is there
pressing the button

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:30.360
with our head of European
customer team, PH.

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:32.720
So he and PH are opening
up the first region

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:33.720
in the Netherlands.

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:35.820
That was just a
couple of weeks ago.

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.280
On the right-hand side
is really what we want

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:41.280
to do for every region launch.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.030
On the right side
of the picture,

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:45.840
you see two members of
our product team, Maya

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.600
and Miles Ward.

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:49.960
To Miles's right,
you see Chris Klayko,

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:53.070
who is in charge of North
American customer team.

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:55.854
In the middle, you have
the mayor of Montreal,

00:21:55.854 --> 00:21:57.270
because building
regions has a lot

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:00.440
to do with the entire
region, not just about

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:02.670
the data center that's there.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:06.150
To the mayor's right, we've got
two of our bigger customers.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:08.250
And then to their right
is our country head

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:10.320
of Canada for Google Cloud.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.000
So we take opening up these
new regions very seriously.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.970
It's more than just
a rack of compute.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:19.080
It's about rooms and room and
room of people that are there

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:22.440
to help you with your problems,
to help sell you Cloud,

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.939
and to help you
solve your problems.

00:22:24.939 --> 00:22:27.230
DAVE STIVER: I don't want to
set the wrong expectation.

00:22:27.230 --> 00:22:30.375
But just in 2017, we launched
almost one region a month,

00:22:30.375 --> 00:22:32.010
10 new regions in 2017.

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:34.620
We didn't quite get to one a
month, but we almost got there.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:38.350
So far in 2018, we're almost
already doing two a month.

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:40.170
The Netherlands and
Montreal launched

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:41.650
within a day of one another.

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:43.830
So we're setting
a high bar already

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:45.930
in the first month of 2018.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:48.120
I don't know if we can
keep up, but we'll try.

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:49.536
NEAL MUELLER: It's
fun to work on.

00:22:49.536 --> 00:22:50.286
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:22:50.286 --> 00:22:51.785
NEAL MUELLER: Let's
talk about where

00:22:51.785 --> 00:22:53.055
we're going to build next.

00:22:53.055 --> 00:22:53.805
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:22:53.805 --> 00:22:56.550
I mentioned this at the
beginning of the discussion.

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.240
We're not done yet.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:00.150
We're thinking about
lots of locations.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:01.590
If you look at a
map of the world,

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:03.000
which I stare at all the time--

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:04.816
I spend 50% of my
time, really, talking

00:23:04.816 --> 00:23:06.690
to customers in different
parts of the world,

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:09.480
trying to figure out what they
need, and then the other 50%

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:10.980
figuring out how
do we do the launch

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.970
in this particular location.

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:16.080
So we would love
to hear from you.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:18.150
Here are some spots
that we're already in

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.790
and then some spots that
we're thinking about landing.

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:23.090
You can expect to see
additional locations

00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:24.840
and markets we've
already gone to, like we

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:27.630
launched Osaka in Japan.

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:28.967
NEAL MUELLER: So Alaska, Hawaii.

00:23:28.967 --> 00:23:31.050
That's just because we
highlighted America, right?

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:32.010
DAVE STIVER: Correct.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:33.180
NEAL MUELLER: Got it.

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:34.930
DAVE STIVER: And with
the Montreal launch,

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:36.030
we now have Canada.

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.730
But there are other parts of
the world that still need GCP.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:43.731
And so my ask to you is
take a look at that link.

00:23:43.731 --> 00:23:46.090
NEAL MUELLER: Bit.ly/gcpregions.

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:48.090
It's a Google form
that you can fill out,

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:49.572
and it comes right
to Dave and I.

00:23:49.572 --> 00:23:51.030
And you can tell
us what you think.

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:51.780
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:53.670
Tell us where you want
us to go, and tell us

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:55.322
what we should bring
when we get there.

00:23:55.322 --> 00:23:56.280
NEAL MUELLER: Oh, yeah.

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:58.290
So where do we want
to open a region?

00:23:58.290 --> 00:24:00.664
Where do they want us to open
a region, and what services

00:24:00.664 --> 00:24:02.730
do they want us to
put there, knowing

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:06.290
that Spanner and Bigtable
and the other one

00:24:06.290 --> 00:24:07.760
is going to all be there.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:09.900
DAVE STIVER: BigQuery
already coming.

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.470
But there are a lot
of services in GCP.

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:13.350
It's a big stack.

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:16.380
So I could always use help
from folks prioritizing

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:18.152
what should come next.

00:24:18.152 --> 00:24:18.860
NEAL MUELLER: OK.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:23.900
That is the last slide.

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:25.880
We are now ready to
answer questions.

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:26.755
DAVE STIVER: Perfect.

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.560
NEAL MUELLER: Stay
tuned for the live Q&amp;A.

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:34.070
It will begin in
about one minute.

00:25:02.564 --> 00:25:03.230
DAVE STIVER: OK.

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:04.540
We've got our questions.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:05.974
You want me to dive in first?

00:25:05.974 --> 00:25:08.140
NEAL MUELLER: How about I
read them, and you answer?

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:09.740
DAVE STIVER: Even better.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:11.490
NEAL MUELLER: Questions
from the audience.

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:13.190
Thank you so much for
leaving questions.

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:17.010
If you don't see your
question answered by us now,

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:19.490
you can go ahead and tweet
either one of us on Twitter,

00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:23.750
or you can go and fill out
that bit.ly/gcpregions.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:26.420
There's probably a thing at the
bottom that says leave a note.

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:27.878
That note will come
directly to us,

00:25:27.878 --> 00:25:29.521
so easy to get a hold of us.

00:25:29.521 --> 00:25:30.020
OK.

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:30.944
First question.

00:25:30.944 --> 00:25:33.110
How many total regions are
you planning on building?

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:35.127
DAVE STIVER: 32.

00:25:35.127 --> 00:25:36.710
NEAL MUELLER: 42 is
the answer, right?

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:38.168
"Hitchhiker's Guide
to the Galaxy."

00:25:38.168 --> 00:25:39.830
DAVE STIVER: Yes.

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:41.720
The actual-- I get
this question a lot

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:43.310
even internally at Google.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:45.170
When do we stop?

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:47.000
The answer is when
customers really

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.167
stop asking us for new regions.

00:25:49.167 --> 00:25:51.500
We look-- and this is combined
with that second question

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:54.120
as well, is how do you choose
where to build regions.

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:56.270
So it's all based
on customer demand.

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:58.680
We look at the attributes
that customers want from us,

00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:01.160
whether it's
latency, whether it's

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:04.185
where they can store their
data, how many locations

00:26:04.185 --> 00:26:06.740
do they need to meet their
availability guidelines, which

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:08.390
varies by industry.

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:10.280
We have a lot of customers
that we've already

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:11.930
on-boarded onto
cloud, but we know

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:14.480
it's not an easy journey,
particularly if you're

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:17.150
in an industry that has a lot
of regulatory requirements

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:18.521
that you need to work around.

00:26:18.521 --> 00:26:21.020
That's why we're bringing these
regions to you-- to meet you

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.540
where you're at geographically,
and also in the state

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:24.440
that you're in.

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:26.400
And so to answer
the first question,

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:27.710
we don't have a number.

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:31.160
We'll build until
customers tell us to stop.

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:32.201
NEAL MUELLER: Nice.

00:26:32.201 --> 00:26:32.700
That's good.

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:33.450
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:35.220
If we're putting
numbers on, I think

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:38.439
we'll build less than 100.

00:26:38.439 --> 00:26:39.230
NEAL MUELLER: Yeah.

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:39.938
That makes sense.

00:26:39.938 --> 00:26:41.980
We have 100 points of
presence around the world.

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:44.814
So in the expanse of time, we
could imagine having a region

00:26:44.814 --> 00:26:45.980
on top of each one of those.

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:47.396
But I imagine over
time, those get

00:26:47.396 --> 00:26:49.432
re-shifted around as
customers demand them

00:26:49.432 --> 00:26:50.390
in different locations.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:51.440
So yeah.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:53.420
DAVE STIVER: And the
definition of locations

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:55.880
will also continue to
grow as well, I think.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:58.370
We'll get closer and
closer to customers.

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:00.950
As we've discovered how
important latency is in a lot

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:02.489
of the applications
that we build,

00:27:02.489 --> 00:27:05.030
I think the market will start
to understand and have a better

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:07.040
appreciation for how
important that is,

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:10.130
and be able to pick what parts
of their applications need

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:12.747
to be super close to customers
and which ones don't.

00:27:12.747 --> 00:27:15.080
NEAL MUELLER: The definition
of region might change too.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:15.390
DAVE STIVER: Absolutely.

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:17.020
NEAL MUELLER: It might get
closer and closer to customers.

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:17.830
DAVE STIVER: Yep.

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:18.538
NEAL MUELLER: OK.

00:27:18.538 --> 00:27:19.640
Question number 2.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:21.950
How do you choose
where to build regions?

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:23.480
DAVE STIVER: That one I covered.

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:25.710
We pick it all based
on customer demand.

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:27.680
So it's constantly out
talking to the market,

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:29.360
figuring out what
they need, then

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:33.480
prioritizing, really, based
on the needs of our customers.

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:36.380
We have a huge customer
base all over the world.

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:40.070
A lot of our customers
are global enterprises.

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:43.590
So we find their needs are
expansive all over the world.

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:47.300
So they're looking for us to do
that work, all the data center

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:52.640
work, in order to give them
that single GCP API everywhere.

00:27:52.640 --> 00:27:55.100
NEAL MUELLER: So I guess the
answer to that question is

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:57.110
bit.ly/gcpregions.

00:27:57.110 --> 00:27:59.780
Fill out the form,
Dave and I find out.

00:27:59.780 --> 00:28:02.990
Question number 3-- what changed
in your building strategy

00:28:02.990 --> 00:28:05.620
to enable you to
launch regions faster?

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:06.121
Automation.

00:28:06.121 --> 00:28:07.911
DAVE STIVER: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:28:07.911 --> 00:28:09.680
Automation is the first part.

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:11.600
NEAL MUELLER: Can you
dig in to one element

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:15.470
that I might not have expected
is included within automation?

00:28:15.470 --> 00:28:16.220
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.260
There are actually two
answers to this question.

00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:21.590
The first part is automation,
so getting repeatability.

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:24.710
So we want to stamp these things
out and get the same footprint

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:27.320
everywhere so that
we're not re-engineering

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:28.680
in our architecture.

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:30.890
So we use the same
architecture everywhere.

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:31.700
That's key.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:32.783
NEAL MUELLER: Homogeneity.

00:28:32.783 --> 00:28:33.560
DAVE STIVER: Yep.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:37.700
The second piece is we actually
had to change the way we did

00:28:37.700 --> 00:28:42.500
data center infrastructure
because we have several huge,

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:47.480
one-billion-customer
applications inside of Google--

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.470
search, ads, Gmail.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:51.560
These are huge services
inside of Google

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:53.060
that are all cloud customers.

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:56.180
They're all built on Google's
data center infrastructure.

00:28:56.180 --> 00:29:00.450
Not every customer that we have
in public cloud is that size.

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:03.470
So we had to really look at
how do we tailor Google down

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:05.990
to the needs of our customers
and really shrink it

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:08.390
so we could quickly
bring the technologies

00:29:08.390 --> 00:29:10.370
that they were
interested in to market

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:11.840
and then build out gradually.

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:14.300
And that's one of the
key differences as well.

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:18.710
If we had to bring all
of Google, every part--

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:21.402
if you need to run
search to a region--

00:29:21.402 --> 00:29:23.110
NEAL MUELLER: Every
service, for example.

00:29:23.110 --> 00:29:23.540
DAVE STIVER: Yes.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:25.160
It would take much
longer to do this.

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:27.326
So we really pared that
down and developed something

00:29:27.326 --> 00:29:28.670
that was specific to cloud.

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:31.460
It doesn't forget any of
the pieces that GCP needs,

00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:33.250
but it was tailored
uniquely for cloud.

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:36.110
It's a kind of investment
in something specific

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:39.359
to a particular PA in Google
that I haven't seen before.

00:29:39.359 --> 00:29:41.900
NEAL MUELLER: And that's maybe
why every region to begin with

00:29:41.900 --> 00:29:43.880
doesn't have every
single service.

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:46.430
We're catering to the customers
that have demanded which

00:29:46.430 --> 00:29:49.010
services, which is why it's
so important that customers go

00:29:49.010 --> 00:29:51.710
to bit.ly/gcpregions and
tell us what they want.

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:52.910
DAVE STIVER: Please.

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:55.460
NEAL MUELLER: Number 4--

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:59.770
is there an equivalent to GCping
for competitors' data centers?

00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:03.680
Yeah.

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:05.490
There's one for Azure.

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:08.290
But I don't know that
there's one for Amazon.

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:11.880
I also haven't seen one for
any of the other public clouds.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:15.040
If you find me on Twitter
and ask me this question,

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:16.739
I will try to find
out your answer.

00:30:16.739 --> 00:30:18.280
I think it's an
interesting question.

00:30:18.280 --> 00:30:19.738
I would love to
see one that showed

00:30:19.738 --> 00:30:22.050
all of the public clouds.

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:23.717
Here's why that
would be interesting.

00:30:23.717 --> 00:30:26.050
That would be interesting
because you'll be able to see,

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:29.260
at your spot in my home
state of Minnesota,

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:31.710
which is the closest cloud
region to me physically.

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:32.839
That might be interesting.

00:30:32.839 --> 00:30:34.380
It wouldn't be
interesting because it

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:38.490
wouldn't take account of
going over private networks.

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:40.900
For example, if you were
using our private network,

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:43.350
GCP is the largest private
network in the world.

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:45.510
You have access to it
every time you use GCP.

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:47.910
You don't have access to
it if you're not using GCP.

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:50.670
And so not always does
proximity in terms

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:52.860
of pings over public
transport equate

00:30:52.860 --> 00:30:55.404
to alacrity of actual
experience with cloud.

00:30:55.404 --> 00:30:56.070
See what I mean?

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:57.240
It's more complicated.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:59.040
You might want to
actually boot up

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.660
a workload in each
of those clouds,

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:01.990
and then do it, and
then go over the-- it

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:03.100
would be more complicated.

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:03.800
Yeah, tweet me.

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:05.920
I'll find you an answer.

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:08.850
Number 5-- what are the
metrics or features that

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:12.210
determine a region, and
who are the people that

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:14.490
are involved in this process?

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:16.134
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:31:16.134 --> 00:31:17.550
I guess if you
think about what it

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:20.610
is we use to decide whether we
go-- what triggers, I think,

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:23.640
is the question here, what
triggers a new region.

00:31:23.640 --> 00:31:26.880
For us, it's about
product market fit.

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:29.760
So I have a product that I
can take to market today.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.190
I have a new region and
that set of products

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:33.700
that I'm launching with today.

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:36.180
And we make a minimum
infrastructure investment,

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:37.770
so we have to see
that there's demand

00:31:37.770 --> 00:31:39.910
in the market for that
kind of investment.

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.976
And so we bring
together our sales team

00:31:41.976 --> 00:31:43.350
that's out working
with customers

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:46.830
to assess their demand in that
location for new customers.

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:49.110
We're talking a lot to
our existing customers,

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:51.540
these global multinationals
who are looking

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:54.630
to spread their infrastructure
or their applications

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:55.780
around the world.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:58.530
And then we have all the
internal stakeholders.

00:31:58.530 --> 00:32:02.340
We actually have a number of
internal services at Google

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:04.920
who want to get to
these new regions

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:07.032
as quickly as they can as well.

00:32:07.032 --> 00:32:09.240
So we balance the needs of
all of those stakeholders.

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:10.840
It's not just our
public customers,

00:32:10.840 --> 00:32:13.020
but there are a
lot of internal--

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:15.810
I get emails every day
from Google engineers

00:32:15.810 --> 00:32:19.207
in different services who want
access to these new regions.

00:32:19.207 --> 00:32:20.790
NEAL MUELLER: Because
they want to use

00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:23.190
Compute or Storage or
BigTable or Spanner

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:25.140
that is available publicly
to everybody else,

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.010
but they want to use it for
some of our internal products.

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:27.885
DAVE STIVER: Correct.

00:32:27.885 --> 00:32:28.410
Correct.

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:28.650
NEAL MUELLER: Wow.

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:29.060
That's great.

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:29.425
DAVE STIVER: Yeah.

00:32:29.425 --> 00:32:30.313
NEAL MUELLER: G on G.

00:32:30.313 --> 00:32:31.980
DAVE STIVER: Yes, G on G.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:33.390
NEAL MUELLER: Nice.

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:35.070
Those are the five questions.

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.500
Thank you all so
much for the time.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:39.270
This was the third
of three webinars.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:42.270
We do these webinars
every single Tuesday,

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:45.480
and they keep getting bigger
and better and more interesting.

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:47.400
So tune in next
Tuesday, and learn

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:50.520
more about what's
hot and new on GCP.

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:51.085
Thanks a lot.

00:32:51.085 --> 00:32:51.960
DAVE STIVER: Awesome.

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:52.560
Thanks, Neal.

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:53.690
See you at the
next region launch.

00:32:53.690 --> 00:32:55.690
NEAL MUELLER: See you at
